{\b\cf1 About CALSS\par}
CALSS (Code Authorship Likelihood Scoring System) is an academic decision-support tool. It analyzes students' C programming submissions (.c files) for code similarity and coding-style (authorship-related) anomalies, and lists the submissions that may need a closer look by the instructor.\par
\sa120\par\pard\sa60
{\b\cf1 Important limitation\par}
CALSS does {\b not} establish plagiarism, cheating or misconduct. A flagged pair or a score is a reason to review the work, not a finding. Results must be interpreted by an instructor together with other relevant academic evidence.\par
\sa120\par\pard\sa60
{\b\cf1 Intended users\par}
Authorized instructors and university personnel. For this deployment, CALSS is installed only on approved instructor/admin computers in programming laboratories, not on student computers.\par
\sa120\par\pard\sa60
{\b\cf1 System requirements\par}
\pard\fi-200\li200\sa40
\bullet\tab 64-bit Windows 10 or Windows 11 (Setup does not run on other versions).\par
\bullet\tab About 10 MB of disk space for the program, plus space for the submissions you analyze and the reports you create.\par
\bullet\tab No other software to install. Minimum processor and memory (RAM) requirements have not been formally measured.\par
\bullet\tab Installing for all users needs administrator approval once. CALSS itself runs without administrator rights.\par
\pard\sa60\sa120\par\pard\sa60
{\b\cf1 Optional online features\par}
The core analysis works offline and without either feature.\par
\pard\fi-200\li200\sa40
\bullet\tab {\b Google Classroom sync} needs internet access and a Google account that you authorize in your browser. In this build only Google accounts approved by the CALSS team can sign in.\par
\bullet\tab {\b Gemini AI explanations} need internet access and your own Google Gemini API key. When used, code from up to 50 flagged pairs per run is sent to Google's Gemini service.\par
\pard\sa60\sa120\par\pard\sa60
{\b\cf1 Where CALSS keeps data\par}
\pard\fi-200\li200\sa40
\bullet\tab Classroom downloads: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\CALSS\\Classroom (per Windows user; deleted when that user uninstalls CALSS).\par
\bullet\tab Settings and the last session (folder path, class and student names; no scores or code): %LOCALAPPDATA%\\CALSS (per Windows user).\par
\bullet\tab Gemini key and Google sign-in: Windows Credential Manager, entries starting with "CALSS/" (per Windows user).\par
\bullet\tab Similarity reports: Documents\\CALSS Reports. Imported files: Documents\\CALSS Imports. CSV exports: wherever you save them.\par
\bullet\tab Audit log, shared by all users of this computer: C:\\ProgramData\\CALSS\\audit.log.\par
\bullet\tab CALSS may add a small index file (calss_sync_manifest.jsonl) to a folder it analyzes.\par
\pard\sa60 Uninstalling keeps reports, imports, settings, the audit log and saved sign-ins.\par
\sa120\par\pard\sa60
{\b\cf1 Privacy and responsible use\par}
Submissions, reports and the audit log can contain student names and academic work. Follow your university's data-handling policies. Do not share submissions, reports, API keys or sign-ins, and delete data you no longer need.\par
\sa120\par\pard\sa60
{\b\cf1 Shared Windows accounts\par}
Anyone who signs in to the same Windows account can use that account's CALSS data, saved Gemini key, Google sign-in and last session. Windows Credential Manager does not separate people who share one Windows account. Where possible, each instructor should use a separate, authorized Windows account.\par
\sa120\par\pard\sa60
{\b\cf1 Installation permissions\par}
Install CALSS only as approved by the laboratory administrator, from the official distribution, after checking the installer's SHA-256 checksum. CALSS is not code-signed, so Windows may show a SmartScreen warning, and an all-users installation asks for administrator approval (UAC). Administrative approval of this deployment does not remove these Windows prompts; follow your university IT policy.\par
}
